      "role": "user",
      "content": "Hey, I've got a heifer that just came out of rumenotomy about 6 hours ago. I need a clear protocol for what I should be looking out for over the next few days regarding the incision site and any internal signs of trouble or infection. Also, what's the plan for getting her back on feed?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "For the next 24-48 hours, closely monitor the incision site for signs of swelling, redness, increased pain, or discharge. Pay particular attention to the edges of the incision, which may exhibit redness or discoloration. Internally, watch for signs of ileus, fluid accumulation, or increased abdominal pressure. Take rectal temperatures daily and check nosodes (blood clots) for any unusual changes, such as presence of blood or seepage.\n\nPrioritizing her return to normal nutrition, consider the following plan: \n- Offer corrective medication as per the vet's instructions.\n- Place an Elizabethan device (E-collar) at the incision site to prevent bloat and self-trauma.\n- Provide an easily accessible mineral supplement to prevent deficiencies.\n- Once the vet gives clearance, resuming a normal feeding schedule is crucial for her overall health.",
